    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <iostream>\\n#include <cstdio>\\n#include <cstdlib>\\n#include <climits>\\n#include <omp.h>\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n, s;\\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\\n\\n    // Allocate aligned flat matrix (64-byte alignment for SIMD)\\n    int* mat = nullptr;\\n    if (posix_memalign((void**)&mat, 64, static_cast<size_t>(n) * n * sizeof(int)) != 0) {\\n        return 1;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Read input\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n            scanf(\\\"%d\\\", &mat[i * n + j]);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    const int np1 = n + 1;\\n    // Allocate aligned prefix sum array\\n    long long* pref = nullptr;\\n    if (posix_memalign((void**)&pref, 64, static_cast<size_t>(np1) * np1 * sizeof(long long)) != 0) {\\n        free(mat);\\n        return 1;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Initialize first row and column to zero\\n    for (int j = 0; j < np1; ++j) pref[j] = 0LL;\\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) pref[i * np1] = 0LL;\\n\\n    // First pass: horizontal cumulative sums per row\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        long long row_sum = 0;\\n        const int base_i = (i + 1) * np1;\\n        const int mat_base = i * n;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n            row_sum += mat[mat_base + j];\\n            pref[base_i + (j + 1)] = row_sum;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Second pass: vertical accumulation\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        const int base_cur = (i + 1) * np1;\\n        const int base_prev = i * np1;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n            pref[base_cur + (j + 1)] += pref[base_prev + (j + 1)];\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Search for maximum SxS submatrix sum using OpenMP parallel collapse(2)\\n    int mxSum = INT_MIN;\\n    const int limit = n - s;  // inclusive upper bound\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum)\\n    for (int i = 0; i <= limit; ++i) {\\n        for (int j = 0; j <= limit; ++j) {\\n            long long sum = pref[(i + s) * np1 + (j + s)]\\n                          - pref[i * np1 + (j + s)]\\n                          - pref[(i + s) * np1 + j]\\n                          + pref[i * np1 + j];\\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = static_cast<int>(sum);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%d\\\\n\\\", mxSum);\\n\\n    free(mat);\\n    free(pref);\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/solution.cpp\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Integrating contiguous storage with aligned allocation (from B), two-pass integral image construction (from A), and OpenMP parallel collapse(2) reduction over all starting positions (from C) yields a correct and fast solution.\",\n  \"notes\": \"This integration combines the best aspects of all three source transformations. It uses contiguous storage and aligned allocation for cache efficiency, two-pass integral image construction for O(1) submatrix sums, and OpenMP parallelization over all starting positions to maximize parallelism. The collapse(2) clause is applied correctly on a double loop, avoiding previous compilation errors.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\n    \"08422dc8de5640f3\",\n    \"d9e0ea6d9d8947f1\",\n    \"6d295b5f520242c0\"\n  ],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Flatten matrix from vector-of-vectors to contiguous 1D array\",\n    \"Replace sliding window with integral image for O(1) submatrix sum queries\",\n    \"Two-pass prefix computation: row-wise cumulative sums then vertical accumulation\",\n    \"Use long long for intermediate sums to avoid overflow\",\n    \"OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) over all starting positions\",\n    \"Aligned allocation using posix_memalign (64-byte alignment)\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We are in the integration role (G) for experiment 2acaa15883d54377. We have three source transformations from IDs: 08422dc8de5640f3 (A), d9e0ea6d9d8947f1 (B), and 6d295b5f520242c0 (C). The goal is to integrate diverse archived transformations into a single correct, fast program.\n\nWe have the current source file src/solution.cpp which is the baseline. We need to produce an integrated version that combines the best ideas from A, B, C while ensuring correctness and speed.\n\nFrom history:\n- Previous integration attempts: \n  - Experiment 9f710ec83c994bfa (score 0.6922, correct) combined sources [3359e6233a234b02? Actually it was a different set] but we don't have that source ID in our list. That attempt used integral image with contiguous storage and OpenMP parallel collapse(2). It was successful.\n  - Experiment 2d6bcd3637bf4ad6 (incorrect) combined sources [a69e622158bd4a3d, 9638609624724a27, 6d295b5f520242c0] but failed correctness due to reference output mismatch. That attempt likely had a bug.\n  - Experiment 91d93894760c4ad6 (build_failed) combined sources [08422dc8de5640f3, 6d295b5f520242c0] and had compilation error: \"not enough for loops to collapse\" because they tried to apply collapse(2) on a single loop.\n\nWe need to produce an integration that is correct. The best performing so far was the first attempt (9f710ec83c994bfa) with mean_seconds 0.05984, which used integral image + contiguous storage + OpenMP parallel for collapse(2). That combination seems promising.\n\nNow we have three specific source IDs to integrate:\n- A: Flatten matrix to 1D array, integral image (summed-area table), two-pass prefix computation, loop blocking on search loops.\n- B: Flat aligned array with posix_memalign, combined subtract/add loops into single reduction loop, collapse(2) pragma on initial sum double-loop, OpenMP simd reductions.\n- C: Prefix-sum based O(1) per-submatrix computation, OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) over all starting positions, use long long.\n\nWe need to choose which transformations to retain. The goal is a correct and fast program. We should combine the best aspects:\n\nFrom A:\n- Flatten matrix to contiguous 1D array (good cache locality)\n- Integral image with two-pass prefix computation (efficient O(1) submatrix sum)\n- Loop blocking on search loops? But if we use OpenMP parallel for collapse(2), loop blocking might interfere. We can skip blocking or keep it but then need to ensure correctness with parallelism.\n\nFrom B:\n- Aligned allocation using posix_memalign (64-byte alignment) could improve SIMD.\n- Combined subtract/add loops into single reduction loop: not needed if we use integral image because no sliding window.\n- collapse(2) pragma on initial sum double-loop: but we are replacing that with prefix sum construction, so not directly applicable. However, we can apply simd or parallel to the prefix sum construction loops.\n\nFrom C:\n- Prefix-sum based O(1) per-submatrix computation (same as A's integral image)\n- OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) over all starting positions.\n- Use long long for intermediate sums.\n\nThe key is to produce a correct program. The previous successful integration used: contiguous storage, two-pass prefix sum construction, and then OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) on the search loops with reduction(max). That worked well.\n\nWe should replicate that approach but ensure we incorporate alignment from B if beneficial, though it may not be necessary. Also, we need to avoid compilation errors like \"not enough for loops to collapse\". The error occurred when they tried to apply collapse(2) on a single loop (the search loop after blocking). So we must only use collapse(2) where there are exactly two nested loops.\n\nPlan:\n- Use contiguous 1D array storage (from A and B).\n- Build integral image using two-pass method: first horizontal cumulative sums, then vertical accumulation. This is efficient.\n- For the search over all top-left corners of SxS submatrices, use OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) on the double loop over i and j (starting rows and columns). No blocking needed because parallelism already distributes work.\n- Use long long for prefix sums to avoid overflow.\n- Ensure correct indexing: pref size is (n+1)*(n+1), with row-major order. Indexing formula: pref[i * np1 + j] where np1 = n+1.\n- For the submatrix sum: sum = pref[(i+s)*np1 + (j+s)] - pref[i*np1 + (j+s)] - pref[(i+s)*np1 + j] + pref[i*np1 + j].\n- Use int for mxSum but compute with long long and cast.\n- Include <omp.h> for OpenMP, <algorithm> for std::max? Not needed if we use reduction(max) in parallel loop. But we need to initialize mxSum appropriately (e.g., LLONG_MIN or -2147483648). Since result fits 32-bit signed int, we can start with INT_MIN.\n- Use scanf for input reading into the flat array.\n\nWe should also consider alignment: using posix_memalign from B could improve performance. But it adds complexity and might not be necessary if the compiler aligns automatically. However, to integrate all three sources, we can include aligned allocation as a transformation from B. We'll allocate with 64-byte alignment for both mat and pref arrays.\n\nBut careful: The previous integration attempt that used posix_memalign (from B) was part of experiment 2d6bcd3637bf4ad6 which failed correctness. That failure might have been due to other issues, not necessarily the aligned allocation. We can include it but ensure correct usage.\n\nAlso, from A: loop blocking on search loops - we will skip that because OpenMP parallelization already distributes work and blocking may interfere with collapse(2). The successful integration (9f710ec83c994bfa) did not use blocking; it used direct double loop with collapse(2).\n\nThus the transformations to retain:\n- Flatten matrix from vector-of-vectors to contiguous 1D array (from A)\n- Replace sliding window with integral image for O(1) submatrix sum queries (from A and C)\n- Two-pass prefix computation: row-wise cumulative sums then vertical accumulation (from A)\n- Use long long for intermediate sums (from C)\n- OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) over all starting positions (from C)\n- Aligned allocation with posix_memalign (64-byte alignment) (from B)\n\nWe should also include <cstdlib> for posix_memalign and free, and <climits> or <limits> for INT_MIN.\n\nNow write the code. We'll produce a single file src/solution.cpp.\n\nLet's construct:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n\n    // Allocate aligned flat matrix (64-byte alignment for SIMD)\n    int* mat = nullptr;\n    if (posix_memalign((void**)&mat, 64, static_cast<size_t>(n) * n * sizeof(int)) != 0) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Read input\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i * n + j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    const int np1 = n + 1;\n    // Allocate aligned prefix sum array\n    long long* pref = nullptr;\n    if (posix_memalign((void**)&pref, 64, static_cast<size_t>(np1) * np1 * sizeof(long long)) != 0) {\n        free(mat);\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize first row and column to zero\n    for (int j = 0; j < np1; ++j) pref[j] = 0LL;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) pref[i * np1] = 0LL;\n\n    // First pass: horizontal cumulative sums per row\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        const int base_i = (i + 1) * np1;\n        const int mat_base = i * n;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            row_sum += mat[mat_base + j];\n            pref[base_i + (j + 1)] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Second pass: vertical accumulation\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        const int base_cur = (i + 1) * np1;\n        const int base_prev = i * np1;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            pref[base_cur + (j + 1)] += pref[base_prev + (j + 1)];\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Search for maximum SxS submatrix sum using OpenMP parallel collapse(2)\n    int mxSum = INT_MIN;\n    const int limit = n - s;  // inclusive upper bound\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= limit; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j <= limit; ++j) {\n            long long sum = pref[(i + s) * np1 + (j + s)]\n                          - pref[i * np1 + (j + s)]\n                          - pref[(i + s) * np1 + j]\n                          + pref[i * np1 + j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = static_cast<int>(sum);\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n\n    free(mat);\n    free(pref);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nCheck correctness: The prefix sum construction is correct. The search loop uses collapse(2) on two perfectly nested loops, which should compile fine.\n\nWe need to ensure that the reduction works correctly with int mxSum and long long comparisons; we cast only when updating mxSum. That's fine because result fits in 32-bit signed int.\n\nNow produce JSON output. We must include sources_kept: all three IDs since we are integrating them. The transformations list should describe what we retained from each source.\n\nWe'll write the hypothesis, transformations, notes.\n\nHypothesis: \"Integrating contiguous storage with aligned allocation (from B), two-pass integral image construction (from A), and OpenMP parallel collapse(2) reduction over all starting positions (from C) yields a correct and fast solution.\"\n\nTransformations:\n- Flatten matrix from vector-of-vectors to contiguous 1D array\n- Replace sliding window with integral image for O(1) submatrix sum queries\n- Two-pass prefix computation: row-wise cumulative sums then vertical accumulation\n- Use long long for intermediate sums to avoid overflow\n- OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:) over all starting positions\n- Aligned allocation using posix_memalign (64-byte alignment)\n\nSources_kept: [\"08422dc8de5640f3\", \"d9e0ea6d9d8947f1\", \"6d295b5f520242c0\"]\n\nFiles: only src/solution.cpp with the content above.\n\nNotes: \"This integration combines the best aspects of all three source transformations. It uses contiguous storage and aligned allocation for cache efficiency, two-pass integral image construction for O(1) submatrix sums, and OpenMP parallelization over all starting positions to maximize parallelism. The collapse(2) clause is applied correctly on a double loop, avoiding previous compilation errors.\"\n\nNow produce the JSON response."
